1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,240
Welcome to School Physics, Explained.

2
00:00:02,690 --> 00:00:08,369
This is High School Physics, Unit 3,
Forces and Newton's Laws, Part 2. In this

3
00:00:08,369 --> 00:00:10,626
video, we'll cover five topics.

4
00:00:11,076 --> 00:00:12,548
Free Body Diagrams.

5
00:00:12,998 --> 00:00:15,131
Weight, Normal Force and Apparent Weight.

6
00:00:15,581 --> 00:00:16,392
Friction.

7
00:00:16,842 --> 00:00:18,058
Inclined Planes.

8
00:00:18,508 --> 00:00:20,919
Tension, Pulleys and Connected Objects.

9
00:00:21,369 --> 00:00:22,585
Let's get started.

10
00:00:24,233 --> 00:00:28,393
Real situations are messy: ropes, ramps,
people, tables.

11
00:00:28,843 --> 00:00:34,582
A free body diagram strips all that away,
and shows only the forces on one object.

12
00:00:35,732 --> 00:00:37,076
Here's how to draw one.

13
00:00:37,526 --> 00:00:41,643
First, pick one object and draw it as a
dot or a simple box.

14
00:00:42,093 --> 00:00:47,289
Second, draw an arrow for every force
acting on it, starting from the object and

15
00:00:47,289 --> 00:00:49,368
pointing the way the force acts.

16
00:00:49,818 --> 00:00:53,146
Third, label each arrow, and make bigger
forces longer.

17
00:00:54,296 --> 00:00:59,881
Only include forces acting on the object,
not forces the object exerts on other

18
00:00:59,881 --> 00:01:00,376
things.

19
00:01:00,826 --> 00:01:06,346
And every force needs a source. Gravity
comes from the Earth. Contact forces come

20
00:01:06,346 --> 00:01:08,527
from things touching the object.

21
00:01:09,677 --> 00:01:12,237
Example one: a lamp hanging from a cord.

22
00:01:12,687 --> 00:01:16,484
Gravity pulls the lamp down. The cord
pulls it up with tension.

23
00:01:16,934 --> 00:01:20,924
The lamp isn't accelerating, so the two
arrows are the same length.

24
00:01:22,074 --> 00:01:25,594
Example two: you pull a box to the right
across the floor.

25
00:01:26,044 --> 00:01:30,161
Gravity points down, and the normal force
from the floor points up.

26
00:01:30,611 --> 00:01:34,558
Your pull points right, and friction
points left, against the sliding.

27
00:01:35,008 --> 00:01:39,040
If the box speeds up, the pull arrow is
longer than the friction arrow.

28
00:01:40,190 --> 00:01:44,158
Once the diagram is drawn, add the forces
in each direction.

29
00:01:44,608 --> 00:01:49,303
Say the pull is fifty newtons, and
friction is thirty newtons. The net

30
00:01:49,303 --> 00:01:52,522
horizontal force is twenty newtons to the
right.

31
00:01:52,972 --> 00:01:58,187
Up and down, the normal force and gravity
cancel. So the box accelerates to the

32
00:01:58,187 --> 00:01:58,583
right.

33
00:01:59,733 --> 00:02:01,205
Now try one yourself.

34
00:02:01,655 --> 00:02:07,415
A skydiver falls at a constant speed. Draw
the free body diagram. What can you say

35
00:02:07,415 --> 00:02:08,610
about the forces?

36
00:02:09,060 --> 00:02:10,340
Pause the video now.

37
00:02:10,790 --> 00:02:16,537
There are two forces: gravity down, and
air resistance up. Constant speed means

38
00:02:16,537 --> 00:02:20,539
zero acceleration, so the two forces are
equal in size.

39
00:02:20,839 --> 00:02:25,965
Stand on a scale in an elevator. As the
elevator starts going up, the reading

40
00:02:25,965 --> 00:02:26,364
jumps.

41
00:02:26,814 --> 00:02:29,140
Did you suddenly gain weight? Not at all.

42
00:02:30,290 --> 00:02:34,642
Weight is the force of gravity on you. It
equals your mass times g.

43
00:02:35,092 --> 00:02:40,558
A sixty kilogram student has a weight of
sixty times nine point eight, about five

44
00:02:40,558 --> 00:02:42,110
hundred ninety newtons.

45
00:02:42,560 --> 00:02:47,830
Mass is how much stuff you're made of, in
kilograms. Weight is a force, in newtons.

46
00:02:48,980 --> 00:02:53,673
When you stand on the floor, the floor
pushes up on you. That's the normal force.

47
00:02:54,123 --> 00:02:58,624
Normal means perpendicular. It always
pushes straight out of the surface.

48
00:02:59,074 --> 00:03:04,088
Standing still on level ground, the normal
force exactly balances your weight.

49
00:03:05,238 --> 00:03:09,971
A bathroom scale doesn't measure your
weight directly. It measures the normal

50
00:03:09,971 --> 00:03:12,491
force, how hard it has to push up on you.

51
00:03:12,941 --> 00:03:14,968
We call that reading your apparent weight.

52
00:03:16,118 --> 00:03:21,302
In an elevator speeding up upward at two
meters per second squared, the net force

53
00:03:21,302 --> 00:03:22,198
must point up.

54
00:03:22,648 --> 00:03:25,954
So the normal force minus your weight
equals m a.

55
00:03:26,404 --> 00:03:31,748
The normal force is sixty times nine point
eight plus two, which is about seven

56
00:03:31,748 --> 00:03:34,319
hundred ten newtons. You feel heavier.

57
00:03:35,469 --> 00:03:40,763
When the elevator accelerates downward at
two meters per second squared, the normal

58
00:03:40,763 --> 00:03:41,656
force shrinks.

59
00:03:42,106 --> 00:03:47,349
It's sixty times nine point eight minus
two, about four hundred seventy newtons.

60
00:03:47,349 --> 00:03:48,463
You feel lighter.

61
00:03:48,913 --> 00:03:53,320
And if the cable snapped, you'd fall
together with the elevator. The normal

62
00:03:53,320 --> 00:03:56,081
force would be zero, and you'd feel
weightless.

63
00:03:57,231 --> 00:03:58,703
Now try one yourself.

64
00:03:59,153 --> 00:04:05,245
A fifty kilogram person stands on a scale
in an elevator accelerating upward at one

65
00:04:05,245 --> 00:04:10,310
meter per second squared. Use g equals
ten. What does the scale read?

66
00:04:10,760 --> 00:04:12,040
Pause the video now.

67
00:04:12,490 --> 00:04:18,144
The normal force is fifty times ten plus
one, which is five hundred fifty newtons.

68
00:04:18,444 --> 00:04:23,641
Try to push a heavy couch. At first,
nothing happens. Then suddenly it lurches

69
00:04:23,641 --> 00:04:26,572
forward, and keeping it moving feels
easier.

70
00:04:27,022 --> 00:04:29,433
That's friction, and it comes in two
kinds.

71
00:04:30,583 --> 00:04:35,319
Friction acts along the surface between
two objects, and it always opposes

72
00:04:35,319 --> 00:04:35,831
sliding.

73
00:04:36,281 --> 00:04:40,142
It comes from tiny bumps on the surfaces
catching on each other.

74
00:04:41,292 --> 00:04:44,919
When the object isn't sliding yet, we have
static friction.

75
00:04:45,369 --> 00:04:50,804
Static friction matches your push, up to a
maximum. Push with fifty newtons, and it

76
00:04:50,804 --> 00:04:53,817
pushes back with fifty. The couch stays
still.

77
00:04:54,267 --> 00:05:00,122
Its maximum value is mu s times the normal
force, where mu s is the coefficient of

78
00:05:00,122 --> 00:05:01,264
static friction.

79
00:05:02,414 --> 00:05:05,337
Once the object slides, it's kinetic
friction.

80
00:05:05,787 --> 00:05:11,143
Kinetic friction equals mu k times the
normal force, and it stays about the same

81
00:05:11,143 --> 00:05:12,080
while sliding.

82
00:05:12,530 --> 00:05:18,043
Mu k is usually smaller than mu s. That's
why it's easier to keep something moving

83
00:05:18,043 --> 00:05:19,186
than to start it.

84
00:05:20,336 --> 00:05:26,837
Here's an example. A forty kilogram crate
sits on a floor where mu s is zero point

85
00:05:26,837 --> 00:05:30,960
five and mu k is zero point three. Use g
equals ten.

86
00:05:31,410 --> 00:05:34,887
The normal force equals the weight, four
hundred newtons.

87
00:05:35,337 --> 00:05:40,573
To start it moving, you need more than
zero point five times four hundred, which

88
00:05:40,573 --> 00:05:42,079
is two hundred newtons.

89
00:05:42,529 --> 00:05:47,921
Once it slides, kinetic friction is zero
point three times four hundred, which is

90
00:05:47,921 --> 00:05:49,718
one hundred twenty newtons.

91
00:05:50,868 --> 00:05:55,945
Notice that friction depends on the normal
force, not on the area of contact.

92
00:05:56,395 --> 00:06:01,929
Press down harder, and friction grows.
Rough surfaces, like rubber on concrete,

93
00:06:01,929 --> 00:06:04,801
have big coefficients. Ice has tiny ones.

94
00:06:05,951 --> 00:06:07,423
Now try one yourself.

95
00:06:07,873 --> 00:06:13,823
A ten kilogram box slides across a floor
with mu k equal to zero point two. Use g

96
00:06:13,823 --> 00:06:19,699
equals ten. What is the kinetic friction
force? If you push with thirty newtons,

97
00:06:19,699 --> 00:06:21,462
what's the acceleration?

98
00:06:21,912 --> 00:06:23,192
Pause the video now.

99
00:06:23,642 --> 00:06:29,118
Friction is zero point two times one
hundred, which is twenty newtons. The net

100
00:06:29,118 --> 00:06:34,664
force is thirty minus twenty, ten newtons,
so the acceleration is one meter per

101
00:06:34,664 --> 00:06:35,717
second squared.

102
00:06:36,016 --> 00:06:41,309
Put a box on a ramp. Tilt it a little, and
the box stays put. Tilt it more, and the

103
00:06:41,309 --> 00:06:42,011
box slides.

104
00:06:42,461 --> 00:06:45,767
To understand why, we split gravity into
two parts.

105
00:06:46,917 --> 00:06:52,235
On a ramp, tilt your axes. Let x point
along the slope, and y point straight out

106
00:06:52,235 --> 00:06:53,232
of the surface.

107
00:06:53,682 --> 00:07:00,048
Now the normal force lies along y, and
friction lies along x. Only gravity is at

108
00:07:00,048 --> 00:07:00,765
an angle.

109
00:07:01,915 --> 00:07:03,792
Split gravity into components.

110
00:07:04,242 --> 00:07:09,533
The part along the slope is m g sine
theta. It pulls the box down the ramp.

111
00:07:09,983 --> 00:07:15,828
The part into the surface is m g cosine
theta. It presses the box into the ramp.

112
00:07:16,978 --> 00:07:19,794
Perpendicular to the ramp, nothing
accelerates.

113
00:07:20,244 --> 00:07:26,164
So the normal force equals m g cosine
theta. On a ramp, the normal force is less

114
00:07:26,164 --> 00:07:27,348
than the weight.

115
00:07:28,498 --> 00:07:33,703
Example: a frictionless ramp tilted at
thirty degrees. Use g equals ten.

116
00:07:34,153 --> 00:07:40,260
Along the slope, the net force is m g sine
theta, so the acceleration is g sine

117
00:07:40,260 --> 00:07:40,724
theta.

118
00:07:41,174 --> 00:07:46,276
That's ten times sine thirty, which is
five meters per second squared, down the

119
00:07:46,276 --> 00:07:48,214
ramp. The mass doesn't matter.

120
00:07:49,364 --> 00:07:52,884
With friction, friction points up the
slope, against the sliding.

121
00:07:53,334 --> 00:08:00,502
The net force along the slope is m g sine
theta minus mu k times m g cosine theta.

122
00:08:00,952 --> 00:08:06,435
So the acceleration is g times sine theta
minus mu k cosine theta.

123
00:08:07,585 --> 00:08:09,057
Now try one yourself.

124
00:08:09,507 --> 00:08:15,840
A box slides down a ramp at thirty seven
degrees, where sine is zero point six and

125
00:08:15,840 --> 00:08:21,943
cosine is zero point eight. Mu k is zero
point two five, and g is ten. Find the

126
00:08:21,943 --> 00:08:22,947
acceleration.

127
00:08:23,397 --> 00:08:24,677
Pause the video now.

128
00:08:25,127 --> 00:08:30,961
a equals ten times zero point six minus
zero point two five times zero point

129
00:08:30,961 --> 00:08:37,180
eight. That's ten times zero point four,
which is four meters per second squared.

130
00:08:37,480 --> 00:08:42,740
Ropes, cables and chains let us pull from
a distance, and pulleys let us change the

131
00:08:42,740 --> 00:08:44,008
direction of a pull.

132
00:08:44,458 --> 00:08:48,191
To solve these problems, we need one more
force: tension.

133
00:08:49,341 --> 00:08:53,907
Tension is the pulling force a rope exerts
on whatever it's attached to.

134
00:08:54,357 --> 00:08:59,408
A rope can only pull, never push. The
tension arrow always points along the

135
00:08:59,408 --> 00:09:01,226
rope, away from the object.

136
00:09:01,676 --> 00:09:07,235
For a light rope, the tension is the same
all along it. An ideal pulley just changes

137
00:09:07,235 --> 00:09:08,161
its direction.

138
00:09:09,311 --> 00:09:14,645
Example one: a ten kilogram bucket hangs
from a rope. Use g equals ten.

139
00:09:15,095 --> 00:09:20,646
If the bucket hangs still, the tension
balances its weight. T equals m g, which

140
00:09:20,646 --> 00:09:22,263
is one hundred newtons.

141
00:09:22,713 --> 00:09:27,995
If you pull the bucket up with an
acceleration of two meters per second

142
00:09:27,995 --> 00:09:33,723
squared, T minus m g equals m a. So T is
ten times twelve, one hundred twenty

143
00:09:33,723 --> 00:09:34,318
newtons.

144
00:09:35,468 --> 00:09:40,998
Example two: two blocks are joined by a
string on a frictionless table. A pull of

145
00:09:40,998 --> 00:09:43,660
thirty newtons acts on the front block.

146
00:09:44,110 --> 00:09:48,743
The back block has a mass of two
kilograms, and the front block has a mass

147
00:09:48,743 --> 00:09:49,870
of four kilograms.

148
00:09:50,320 --> 00:09:56,473
Treat them as one system first. The total
mass is six kilograms, so the acceleration

149
00:09:56,473 --> 00:10:01,307
is thirty divided by six, which is five
meters per second squared.

150
00:10:01,757 --> 00:10:07,512
Now look at the back block alone. The only
horizontal force on it is the string's

151
00:10:07,512 --> 00:10:11,421
tension. T equals two times five, which is
ten newtons.

152
00:10:12,571 --> 00:10:17,975
Example three: an Atwood machine. Two
masses hang over a pulley: three kilograms

153
00:10:17,975 --> 00:10:20,272
on one side, and two on the other.

154
00:10:20,722 --> 00:10:25,510
The heavier side goes down. The net
driving force is the difference in

155
00:10:25,510 --> 00:10:29,341
weights: three minus two, times g, which
is ten newtons.

156
00:10:29,791 --> 00:10:35,133
That force moves the total mass of five
kilograms. So the acceleration is two

157
00:10:35,133 --> 00:10:36,937
meters per second squared.

158
00:10:38,087 --> 00:10:41,223
Here's the method for any connected
objects problem.

159
00:10:41,673 --> 00:10:47,287
Draw a free body diagram for each object.
Use the fact that they share the same

160
00:10:47,287 --> 00:10:50,697
acceleration. Then solve the equations
together.

161
00:10:51,847 --> 00:10:53,319
Now try one yourself.

162
00:10:53,769 --> 00:10:59,780
A five kilogram block on a frictionless
table is tied over a pulley to a hanging

163
00:10:59,780 --> 00:11:05,865
block of five kilograms. Use g equals ten.
Find the acceleration and the tension.

164
00:11:06,315 --> 00:11:07,595
Pause the video now.

165
00:11:08,045 --> 00:11:13,622
The driving force is fifty newtons, and
the total mass is ten kilograms, so a is

166
00:11:13,622 --> 00:11:19,059
five meters per second squared. For the
table block, T equals five times five,

167
00:11:19,059 --> 00:11:21,080
which is twenty five newtons.

168
00:11:21,530 --> 00:11:27,119
That completes Unit 3. Next, we'll move in
circles: circular motion and gravity.

169
00:11:28,269 --> 00:11:33,104
If this lesson helped, subscribe to School
Physics, Explained, and tap the bell so

170
00:11:33,104 --> 00:11:34,755
you don't miss the next one.

